## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of two classic sorts, practical optimization, and memory trade-offs. A hybrid approach reveals ability to tune algorithms for real data patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting threshold and when it helps
- Stability via merge vs insertion behavior
- Space usage with a single auxiliary buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
function sortBatch(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const buf = new Array(n);
  function insertionSort(a, left, right) {
    for (let i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
      const key = a[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= left && a[j] > key) {
        a[j + 1] = a[j];
        j--;
      }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function merge(left, mid, right) {
    let i = left, j = mid + 1, k = left;
    while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
      if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
      else buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    }
    while (i <= mid) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
    while (j <= right) buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    for (let t = left; t <= right; t++) arr[t] = buf[t];
  }
  function sort(l, r) {
    if (l >= r) return;
    if (r - l + 1 <= 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, l, r);
    } else {
      const m = Math.floor((l + r) / 2);
      sort(l, m);
      sort(m + 1, r);
      merge(l, m, r);
    }
  }
  sort(0, n - 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests dynamic programming skills on a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with multiple predecessor choices and path counting for optimal solutions. The ability to handle several predecessors, implement proper tie-breaking for path counting, and correctly apply modulo arithmetic demonstrates code clarity and robustness with edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- DP on DAG with 3-step transitions (i → i+1, i+2, i+3)
- Maintaining both the optimal value and the count of optimal paths
- Modulo arithmetic (1e9+7) for path counting
- Edge-case handling for small n and negative profit values

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitAndPaths(profits) {
  const n = profits.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007n;
  if (n === 0) return { best: 0, cnt: 1 };
  if (n === 1) return { best: profits[0], cnt: 1 };

  const best = new Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt = new Array(n).fill(0);
  best[0] = profits[0];
  cnt[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    let maxVal = -Infinity;
    for (const p of [i-1, i-2, i-3]) {
      if (p >= 0) {
        const cand = best[p] + profits[i];
        if (cand > maxVal) {
          maxVal = cand;
          cnt[i] = cnt[p];
        } else if (cand === maxVal) {
          cnt[i] = (cnt[i] + cnt[p]) % MOD;
        }
      }
    }
    best[i] = maxVal;
  }
  return { best: best[n-1], cnt: cnt[n-1] };
}
```

