## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced DP: partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums and both memoized and tabulated solutions. Demonstrates recurrence derivation, base cases, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Time: O(N^2 K); Space: O(NK) (or O(N))
- Edge-case handling for N < K

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, K){
  const N = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<N;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+A[i];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++) dp[i][1]=(pref[i]-pref[0])**2;
  for(let k=2;k<=K;k++){
    for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
      for(let j=k-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + (pref[i]-pref[j])**2);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][K];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you apply divide-and-conquer optimization if the cost function supports it?
- How would you adapt for at-most K segments or large N with memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a clean DP formulation for segmentation with per-placement costs and wildcards. It also probes backtracking and memoization versus tabulation choices under a dictionary constraint.

## Key Concepts
- DP on string positions with a dictionary
- Memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Backtracking reconstruction of segmentation
- Handling wildcards and per-placement costs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCost(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  const choice = new Array(n).fill(null);

  function costAt(i, w) {
    let cost = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < w.length; j++) {
      if (s[i+j] !== '?' && s[i+j] !== w[j]) {
        cost++;
      }
    }
    return cost;
  }

  function dp(i) {
    if (i === n) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== null) return memo[i];

    let best = Infinity;
    let bestWord = null;

    for (const w of dict) {
      if (i + w.length <= n) {
        const c = costAt(i, w) + dp(i + w.length);
        if (c < best) {
          best = c;
          bestWord = w;
        }
      }
    }

    memo[i] = best;
    choice[i] = bestWord;
    return best;
  }

  const minCost = dp(0);
  if (minCost === Infinity) return -1;

  // Reconstruct segmentation
  const words = [];
  let i = 0;
  while (i < n) {
    words.push(choice[i]);
    i += choice[i].length;
  }

  return { cost: minCost, words };
}
```

## Complexity
Time: O(n × sum|w|) where sum|w| is total length of all dictionary words
Space: O(n) for memoization and choice arrays

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of sorting trade-offs (in-place memory vs stability vs constants) and how hybrid approaches balance speed, especially relevant in finance and large-scale data.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning in quicksort
- Insertion sort for small slices
- In-place sorting and O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case complexities; pivot choice

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortHybrid(a){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(i,j)=>{ [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]; };
  const insertSort=(l,r)=>{
    for(let i=l+1;i<=r;i++){
      let x=a[i], j=i-1;
      while(j>=l && a[j]>x){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
      a[j+1]=x;
    }
  };
  const partition=(lo,hi)=>{
    const p=a[lo+((hi-lo)>>1)];
    let i=lo, lt=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(a[i]<p){ swap(i,lt); i++; lt++; }
      else if(a[i]>p){ swap(i,gt); gt--; }
      else i++;
    }
    return [lt,gt];
  };
  const qs=(lo,hi)=>{
    if(lo>=hi) return;
    if(hi-lo+1 <= TH){ insertSort(lo,hi); return; }
    const [l,r] = partition(lo,hi);
    qs(lo, l-1);
    qs(r+1, hi);
  };
  qs(0,a.length-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to blend Trie prefix search with balanced BSTs for dynamic scores, plus a top-k retrieval strategy that scales efficiently. It tests practical update propagation and memory trade-offs in a high-throughput autocomplete system.

## Key Concepts
- Trie for prefix lookup
- AVL/balanced BST per node for phrase-score storage
- Top-k extraction via a front-to-back heap or subtree aggregation
- Update propagation costs and memory considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.words = new AVLTree(); // key: phrase, value: score
    this.topK = new MaxHeap(k); // top-k scores for subtree
  }
}

class AutoCompleteDictionary {
  constructor(k = 10) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.k = k;
  }

  insertPhrase(phrase, score) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of phrase) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.words.insert(phrase, score);
      node.topK.insert(score);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTopK(k);
  }
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis
- insertPhrase: O(L log n) where L is phrase length, n is phrases per prefix
- updateScore: O(L log n) for BST updates plus O(L) for heap propagation
- deletePhrase: O(L log n) for BST removal and heap cleanup
- queryTopK: O(L + k log k) for traversal plus heap extraction

## Memory Trade-offs
- Trie structure: O(N × L) where N is total phrases, L is average length
- BST per node: O(N) total across all nodes
- Top-k heaps: O(N × k) worst-case, but typically O(N) with pruning

## Edge Cases
- Duplicate phrases: update existing entry instead of inserting
- Score ties: use lexicographic ordering as tiebreaker
- Empty prefix: return global top-k from root node
- Large k: consider segment tree for better range queries

## Comparison with Alternatives
- Hash map + sorting: O(1) insert but O(n log n) prefix queries
- Suffix tree: better substring search but higher memory overhead
- Pure Trie with scores: O(L) queries but O(n) updates for score changes
- Hybrid approach (proposed): balances prefix efficiency with dynamic updates

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates the ability to extend classic DP patterns (like the staircase problem) with an additional resource dimension, while also testing space optimization skills and recursive thinking via memoization. It distinguishes candidates who understand both bottom-up and top-down approaches, and who reason through edge cases systematically.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra budget constraint (two-dimensional state: position + accumulated cost)
- Space optimization using rolling arrays to reduce O(n·B) space to O(B)
- Memoization on composite state (i, b) for the recursive variant
- Correct base case handling when starting from a virtual position before index 0

## Code Example
```javascript
const MOD = 1_000_000_007n;

function countWays(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;

  // bottom-up with rolling arrays
  let prev2 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
  let prev1 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);

  // base: step 0
  if (cost[0] <= B) prev2[cost[0]] = 1;

  // base: step 1
  if (n > 1 && cost[1] <= B) prev1[cost[1]] = 1;

  // step 2 onwards
  for (let i = 2; i < n; i++) {
    const cur = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
    for (let b = cost[i]; b <= B; b++) {
      cur[b] = (prev1[b - cost[i]] + prev2[b - cost[i]]) % MOD;
    }
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }

  return n === 1 ? prev2[B] : prev1[B];
}

// memoized DFS variant
function countWaysDFS(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  const memo = new Map();

  function dfs(i, b) {
    if (b < 0) return 0;
    if (i < 0) return b === 0 ? 1 : 0;
    const key = `${i},${b}`;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const result = (dfs(i - 1, b - cost[i]) + dfs(i - 2, b - cost[i])) % MOD;
    memo.set(key, result);
    return result;
  }

  return dfs(n - 1, B);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP state design, memoization vs tabulation, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions; state i is start index; transitions extend end while sum <= limit.
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums; reconstruction via choice pointers.
- Edge cases: impossible when an element exceeds limit; handling ties.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPartitionCost(nums, limit){
  const n = nums.length, pref=[0];
  for(const x of nums) pref.push(pref[pref.length-1]+x);
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  function dfs(i){
    if(i===n) return [0,[]];
    if(memo[i]) return memo[i];
    let best=[Infinity, null];
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){ const sum=pref[j+1]-pref[i]; if(sum>limit) break; const sub=dfs(j+1); const cost=(j-i+1)**2+sub[0]; if(cost<best[0]) best=[cost, [i,j].concat(sub[1]||[])]; }
    memo[i]=best; return best;
  }
  return dfs(0);
}
``` 

