- How does memory scale with the corpus size, and alternatives like compressed tries?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests stability, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs between sorting algorithms. It asks for a concrete approach rather than a generic answer.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sorting
- Bottom-up merge sort
- Time/space complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSort(arr){
  const n = arr.length; 
  const aux = new Array(n);
  for (let width = 1; width < n; width *= 2){
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i += width * 2){
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, n);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, n);
      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right){
        if (arr[p].score <= arr[q].score) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
        else aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      for (let t = left; t < right; t++) arr[t] = aux[t];
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for memory-constrained environments where n is very large?
- Compare stability guarantees and real-time performance between mergesort and quicksort in streaming data.

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to design a compact prefix-search data structure and implement tie-breaking logic using a Trie.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequencies stored at each node
- Prefix search via traversal to the prefix end node
- DFS collection of all words in the subtree
- Sorting by frequency (descending), then lexicographic order for ties

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.freq = 0;
  }
}

class Autocomplete {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  addWord(word, freq) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.freq = freq;
  }

  getSuggestions(prefix, limit) {
    if (limit <= 0) return [];

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    const results = [];
    const dfs = (currentNode, path) => {
      if (currentNode.freq > 0) {
        results.push({ word: path, freq: currentNode.freq });
      }
      for (const [char, childNode] of currentNode.children) {
        dfs(childNode, path + char);
      }
    };

    dfs(node, prefix);

    results.sort((a, b) => {
      if (b.freq !== a.freq) return b.freq - a.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });

    return results.slice(0, limit).map(r => r.word);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests practical DP skills: counting subsets, managing zeros, and comparing memoization vs tabulation.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting
- Top-down memoization
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and zero handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsetsTopDown(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Map());
  function dfs(i, t){
    if (t === 0) return 1;
    if (i === n) return 0;
    const key = t;
    if (memo[i].has(key)) return memo[i].get(key);
    let res = dfs(i+1, t);
    if (t >= nums[i]) res = (res + dfs(i+1, t - nums[i]));
    memo[i].set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}
function countSubsetsBottomUp(nums, target){
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const v of nums){
    for (let w = target; w >= v; w--){
      dp[w] = (dp[w] + dp[w - v]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to handle negative numbers or count modulo large M?
- How would you modify to return counts for multiple target sums simultaneously?
